---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "orgain protein powder",
      "orgain organic protein powder",
      "orgain protein review",
      "orgain plant protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Organic Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-organic-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Orgain Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      The supermarket's best-known organic protein, read off the label: what each Orgain tub gives you per scoop, what it costs per 25 g of protein, and what Consumer Reports found when it tested the flagship for lead.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#the-brand">Who Makes Orgain</a>
      <a href="#range-compared">The Range Side by Side</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#plant-vs-whey">Plant-Based vs Grass-Fed Whey</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#lead-testing">The Lead Result</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners, Fiber and Digestion</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#how-to-use">Matching the Tub to Your Goal</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Orgain is the protein powder most people meet first, because it sits on the shelf at Target, Sam's Club and the grocery store as well as on Amazon. The flagship Organic Protein Plant-Based gives you about 21 g of protein for roughly 150 calories, it is USDA Organic, and it costs less per gram than most organic rivals. It is a sensible everyday smoothie powder. It is not a lifter's powder, it carries no sport certification we could confirm, and Consumer Reports' 2025 lead testing put it above its daily level of concern, so it belongs in the occasional rather than the twice-a-day column.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Orgain is good at one thing: an organic, plant-based protein powder that tastes sweet enough to drink, mixes into a smoothie without much fuss and costs less than most organic competitors. If that is what you want, the flagship <strong>Organic Protein Plant-Based</strong> tub is a reasonable buy. About 21 g of protein for roughly 150 calories, per the label at the time of writing, from a blend of organic pea, brown rice and chia, with under 1 g of sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is weaker on the things serious lifters and tested athletes care about. Twenty-one grams is on the low side for a two-scoop serving, the protein share by weight is under half because fiber, fat and a creamer base take up room, and we could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status for any Orgain powder. If you get drug tested, buy a certified product instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger caveat is lead. In its October 2025 protein investigation, Consumer Reports measured Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein Powder at about 143% of its daily level of concern for lead per serving and grouped it with products it said were fine to have occasionally through the week, not every day. That does not make the tub dangerous. It does change how often we would drink it, and we cover the numbers in full below.
          </p>

          <p>
            So our verdict splits by reader. For a casual smoothie drinker who wants organic, Orgain is fine and good value. For a heavy daily user, especially someone having two shakes a day, the grass-fed whey or a certified organic plant powder is the better primary protein. Pregnant readers and parents buying for children should read the lead section before buying any plant protein, Orgain included.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Good value organic smoothie protein, not an everyday staple</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy the 21 g Organic Protein Plant-Based for occasional smoothies, the Grass-Fed Whey if you want more protein per calorie, and a sport-certified powder if you are tested.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Orgain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Orgain was founded by Dr. Andrew Abraham, a physician who has told the brand's origin story many times: he built the first shakes while recovering from cancer treatment because he could not find a clean nutrition drink he wanted to use. The company started with ready-to-drink shakes and grew into one of the largest organic protein powder brands in the United States.
          </p>

          <p>
            In February 2022 Nestl&eacute; Health Science agreed to buy a majority stake in Orgain from Dr. Abraham and the investment firm Butterfly Equity, both of whom kept minority positions, with Dr. Abraham staying on as chief executive. That puts Orgain inside one of the biggest nutrition companies in the world, alongside brands such as Garden of Life and Vital Proteins that Nestl&eacute; Health Science also owns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Big-company ownership cuts both ways. It is why Orgain is in almost every supermarket and why prices stay competitive, and it usually means mature manufacturing and quality systems. It also means the range is built for mass appeal: sweet flavors, gentle textures and serving sizes that suit a smoothie more than a post-workout shake. Orgain's own pitch is organic, plant-based and clean, not performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            The label claims are consistent across the core range: USDA Organic for the organic lines, vegan for the plant powders, gluten free and non-GMO. Retail listings for the plant-based tub also mention kosher status and certified plant-based verification. What you will not find on the tub is a banned-substance certification, which is the single biggest gap if you compete in a tested sport.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="range-compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Range Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Orgain sells more protein powders than most shoppers realise, and the names are close enough to cause confusion. The four that matter are the flagship 21 g Organic Protein Plant-Based, a higher-dose 30 g organic vegan powder sold as Orgain Sport at some retailers, a Grass-Fed Whey made from cow's milk, and Orgain Simple, a shorter-ingredient plant powder sweetened with coconut sugar. There are also Superfoods and oat milk variants of the flagship.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein source</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Organic Protein Plant-Based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic pea, brown rice, chia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia; erythritol in some flavors</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Organic Vegan 30g (Sport)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 200-210</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic plant blend</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check flavor</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grass-Fed Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass-fed whey concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia, erythritol</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Plant Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pea, peanut, pumpkin, almond, chia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coconut sugar</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pattern is simple. Three of the four land at 20 to 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving, which is a moderate dose; most whey isolates give 25 g in a single scoop. Only the 30 g vegan powder reaches a dose that comfortably covers the roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine that research links to a strong muscle-building response, and it does that by doubling up on serving size and calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flagship's 46 g serving is worth a second look. With 21 g of protein inside 46 g of powder, protein is under half of what you scoop. The rest is fiber, which ranges from about 4 g to 7 g depending on flavor, plus a creamer base of acacia, sunflower oil and rice dextrin, and about 4 g of fat. That is why it tastes richer than a plain pea isolate, and why it is not the leanest protein per calorie.
          </p>

          <p>
            Orgain Simple is the odd one out. It swaps stevia and erythritol for organic coconut sugar, which adds about 5 g of added sugar per serving, and its blend includes peanut flour and almond protein, so it contains peanuts and tree nuts. It suits someone who dislikes stevia aftertaste and does not mind a little real sugar. It does not suit anyone with a nut allergy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Orgain Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked the four Orgain powders most buyers choose between on protein per serving, cost per 25 g, ingredient list and how well each fits its intended job, then added one clearly labelled alternative from another brand for readers who want a sport-certified organic plant protein. All figures are per the label or retailer listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the tub you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="plant-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Plant-Based vs Grass-Fed Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are not vegan and you are choosing between Orgain's two 21 g powders, the Grass-Fed Whey is the stronger protein on paper. It gives about 21 g of protein for roughly 140 calories, and the listing claims close to 5 g of naturally occurring BCAAs per serving. Whey is a complete, fast-digesting dairy protein with more leucine per gram than a pea and rice blend, so the same 21 g does a little more for muscle repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            The whey is a concentrate rather than an isolate, which matters if you are lactose intolerant. Concentrates keep more of milk's lactose and fat than isolates, so sensitive stomachs may do better with a true isolate such as those in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate guide</a>. Orgain's whey is gluten free and kosher per the listing, and is sweetened with stevia and erythritol.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plant tub wins on dietary fit and fiber. It is dairy free and vegan, it carries 4 to 7 g of fiber per serving depending on flavor, and its pea, brown rice and chia blend is designed to cover all nine essential amino acids. Pea and rice complement each other: pea is low in methionine, rice is low in lysine, and together they make a more complete protein than either alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a lead angle here too. Plant proteins grow in soil and tend to carry more lead than dairy proteins, which is consistent with the Orgain plant tub's result in Consumer Reports' testing. If you are choosing purely on lowest contaminant exposure, a dairy protein is the safer default. If you are vegan, choosing a plant powder with published heavy-metal results is the better route, and our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">organic protein powder guide</a> compares the certified options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price per tub is a poor way to compare protein powders, because tubs hold different serving counts and each serving holds a different dose. We compare cost per 25 g of protein instead: the price of the tub, divided by total grams of protein in it, multiplied by 25. Prices move constantly, so treat the figures here as approximate ranges at typical retail and subscribe prices at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flagship plant tub is where Orgain is strongest. A 2.03 lb tub holds about 20 servings of 21 g, and at its usual price range on Amazon and at warehouse clubs it works out to roughly $1.40 to $2.00 per 25 g of protein. That is cheap for a USDA Organic plant powder; many organic rivals sit well above $2. The larger 2.74 lb club-size tubs bring the cost down further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grass-Fed Whey usually lands in a similar band. A 1.82 lb tub holds about 20 servings, and at the discounted prices we saw it works out to roughly $1.50 to $2.20 per 25 g. That is not bargain-bin whey money, but it is fair for grass-fed whey with a short label. The 30 g vegan powder and Simple tend to cost a little more per gram of protein, Simple especially, because its tubs are smaller.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Approximate Cost per 25 g</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.40-2.00</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Organic Plant-Based (2.03 lb)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.50-2.20</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Grass-Fed Whey (1.82 lb)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Higher</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Simple, per gram</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings in a 2.03 lb tub</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One wrinkle: because the plant tub's serving is 21 g, you need about 1.2 servings to reach 25 g, which means a tub lasts fewer shakes than its label suggests if you dose to a muscle-building target. Do the math on grams of protein, not scoops. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will tell you how much you actually need per day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lead-testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Lead Result
          </h2>

          <p>
            In October 2025 Consumer Reports published lead test results for 23 protein powders and ready-to-drink shakes from popular brands. It judged each product against a level of concern of 0.5 micrograms of lead per day, a conservative threshold set well below levels known to cause harm, and sorted products into those it was comfortable with daily, those it said were fine occasionally, and those to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein Powder came in at about 143% of that level of concern per serving and was placed in the occasional group, alongside products such as Vega Premium Sport and Equip Prime Protein. In plain terms, one serving delivered a little more lead than CR thinks is ideal for a whole day. Having it a few times a week keeps your weekly average under CR's threshold; having it twice a day does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Context matters. CR's level of concern is deliberately cautious, protein powder is not the only dietary source of lead, and a single lab test reflects the lots CR bought, not every tub ever made. Lead in plant proteins comes mostly from soil, not from careless manufacturing, which is why rice, pea and cacao-based powders tend to score worse across the whole category. Still, the result is real and it applies to Orgain's best-selling powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our practical advice: if Orgain plant protein is your occasional smoothie powder, keep using it. If you rely on protein shakes daily, use a dairy protein or a plant powder that publishes heavy-metal results as your main source, and keep Orgain for variety. Pregnant readers and children are the groups CR's thresholds exist to protect, so talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before making any plant protein a daily habit. For the bigger picture, see our guide on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sweeteners, Fiber and Digestion
          </h2>

          <p>
            Orgain's flavored powders are sweetened mainly with organic stevia extract, listed as Reb A on recent labels. Several retailer ingredient lists for the plant tub and the whey also include organic erythritol, a sugar alcohol, while a newer vanilla bean listing for the plant tub is marketed as erythritol free. Orgain has told customers that erythritol will always be listed in the ingredients when present, so read the panel on the tub you actually buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters for two reasons. Some people find erythritol causes gas or loose stools in larger amounts, and some readers prefer to avoid it after a 2023 study linked high blood erythritol levels to cardiovascular events. That research is observational and debated, and it does not prove a scoop of protein is harmful, but it is a fair reason to pick an erythritol-free flavor if you care.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fiber is the other digestive variable. The plant tub's 4 to 7 g per serving comes partly from organic acacia and, in some flavors, agave inulin. Inulin feeds gut bacteria, which is good for most people, but it is a common trigger for bloating in people with IBS or a sensitivity to FODMAPs. If plant protein shakes leave you bloated, the inulin and the sugar alcohol are the first suspects, not the pea protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            On taste, buyer feedback is broadly positive for a plant powder: reviewers describe Orgain as creamy and less chalky than plain pea isolates, with chocolate fudge and vanilla bean the most popular flavors. The common complaint is a stevia aftertaste, which is why Simple, with coconut sugar instead, exists. Iced coffee flavor contains about 60 mg of caffeine per serving per the label, so skip it in the evening.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Orgain if you are a drug-tested athlete. We could not confirm an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for any Orgain powder, and a protein powder without banned-substance certification is a risk you do not need to take. A certified organic plant protein such as Garden of Life Sport, which the brand says is NSF Certified for Sport, solves that; our <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life review</a> covers it in depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the plant tub as a primary protein if you drink two or more shakes a day, for the lead reasons above. At that volume your daily lead intake from protein alone would sit well above Consumer Reports' level of concern. Rotating in a dairy protein, or a plant protein with published heavy-metal testing, is the simple fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Orgain if you want the most protein per calorie. At about 21 g for 150 calories, the flagship gives roughly 14 g of protein per 100 calories; a good whey isolate gives 22 to 25 g per 100 calories. If you are dieting and counting every calorie, an isolate stretches further. If you are bulking, the extra calories are not a problem, but you will want the 30 g powder or two servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip Simple if you have a peanut or tree nut allergy, and skip any flavor with erythritol or inulin if those ingredients upset your stomach. Readers with kidney disease, or anyone on a protein-restricted diet, should get a target from their doctor before adding any protein powder. Other plant-first brands worth comparing are covered in our <a href="/vega-protein-powder-review/">Vega review</a> and <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani review</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Matching the Tub to Your Goal
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>For smoothies and general health:</strong> the 21 g Organic Protein Plant-Based is the default. Blend one serving with frozen fruit, a handful of spinach and milk or a plant milk, and the fiber and creamer base give you a thick shake that keeps you full. If you add a dairy milk, you will push the protein past 25 g without needing a second scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For building muscle on a vegan diet:</strong> the 30 g organic vegan powder is the better Orgain pick, because a single serving clears the protein dose most research uses for post-workout recovery. It costs more calories, about 200 to 210 per serving, so it suits lifters and active people more than dieters. Pair it with a daily protein target rather than relying on shakes alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For lean protein if you eat dairy:</strong> the Grass-Fed Whey. It gives the same 21 g as the plant tub for fewer calories, it digests fast after training, and dairy proteins have tested lower for lead than plant proteins across the category. If lactose bothers you, move to a whey isolate instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For short labels and no stevia:</strong> Orgain Simple, as long as you are fine with about 5 g of added coconut sugar and have no nut allergy. Whichever tub you choose, use it as a top-up to real food, not a replacement. Most adults hit their protein target more cheaply from eggs, dairy, beans, fish and meat, with a shake filling the gap on busy days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting scoops instead of grams</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Orgain's flagship serving is two scoops and about 21 g of protein, not 25 g. If you are dosing for muscle repair after training, you need a little more than one serving, and a tub lasts fewer shakes than the label count suggests. Plan your intake in grams of protein, then work back to scoops.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking the plant tub twice a day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Consumer Reports measured the Orgain plant powder at about 143% of its daily lead level of concern per serving. One shake a few times a week stays within its weekly guidance; two shakes daily does not. Heavy users should rotate in a dairy protein or a plant powder with published heavy-metal results.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming organic means tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                USDA Organic certifies how ingredients are grown and handled. It says nothing about banned substances, and it does not cap heavy metals, which come from soil. If you compete in a tested sport, look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport instead, which Orgain powders do not carry as far as we could confirm.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sweetener panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Orgain uses stevia across most flavors, and erythritol appears in some flavors and not others. Inulin fiber shows up in some plant tubs too. If shakes bloat you or give you gas, compare ingredient panels between flavors before blaming the protein itself, and try an erythritol-free flavor first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Simple with a nut allergy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Orgain Simple's current blend includes peanut flour and almond protein, and its label lists peanuts and tree nuts as allergens. It sits next to the nut-free flagship on the shelf in similar packaging. If anyone in the house has a nut allergy, check the allergen line every time you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Orgain protein powder good for you?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, yes, in moderation. The flagship gives about 21 g of organic plant protein for roughly 150 calories with under 1 g of sugar and useful fiber. The caveat is lead: Consumer Reports' 2025 testing put it above its daily level of concern per serving, so we suggest using it a few times a week rather than as a twice-daily staple.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Orgain protein powder have lead?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Consumer Reports' October 2025 investigation measured Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein Powder at about 143% of its 0.5 microgram daily level of concern for lead per serving and listed it as fine to have occasionally through the week. Lead in plant protein comes mainly from soil. Pregnant readers and parents should talk to a doctor before daily use.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Orgain protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The flagship Organic Protein Plant-Based and the Grass-Fed Whey each give about 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving, per the label. Orgain Simple gives about 20 g, and the organic vegan 30g powder, sold as Orgain Sport at some stores, gives about 30 g. Figures vary slightly by flavor, so check the panel on your tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Orgain protein good for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help, but the 21 g serving is on the low side for a post-workout dose, and plant protein has less leucine per gram than whey. Lifters should choose the 30 g vegan powder, the Grass-Fed Whey with a little extra milk, or a higher-dose whey isolate. Total daily protein matters more than any single shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Orgain protein powder contain erythritol?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some flavors do. Retail ingredient lists for several plant-based flavors and the Grass-Fed Whey include organic erythritol alongside stevia, while a newer vanilla bean plant listing is marketed as erythritol free. Orgain says erythritol is always listed when present, so the ingredient panel on the specific tub you buy is the reliable answer.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Orgain third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Orgain's organic and plant-based claims are verified by third parties, and the organic tubs carry the USDA Organic seal. We could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status for any Orgain powder at the time of writing, so drug-tested athletes should choose a powder that appears in one of those certification databases.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who owns Orgain?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Orgain was founded by physician Dr. Andrew Abraham. In February 2022 Nestl&eacute; Health Science agreed to buy a majority stake from Dr. Abraham and investment firm Butterfly Equity, who kept minority stakes, with Dr. Abraham remaining chief executive. Nestl&eacute; Health Science also owns Garden of Life and Vital Proteins, among other nutrition brands.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
